TI  - Feeding plant and fish oils to improve polyunsaturated fat concentrations in intramuscular, perirenal and subcutaneous lambs' fat
T2  - ZUCHTUNGSKUNDE
AB  - The objective of this study was to determine the effects of supplemental plant and fish oils on adipose tissue fatty acids concentration and animal performance in lambs. Twenty four lambs with initial live weight of 20 kg 2 kg were fed one of four diets. Animals were randomly allocated to one of four treatments (n=6): 1) basal diet containing 52% concentrate and 48% meadow hay (CON); 2) basal diet plus 5% of rapeseed and linseed oils blend (RSO/LSO); 3) basal diet plus 5% of linseed and fish oils blend (LSO/FO); 4) basal diet plus 5% of rapeseed, linseed and fish oils blend (RSO/LSO/FO). Adipose tissue samples from subcutaneous, perirenal and intramuscular depots were Iyophilized and analyzed for lipid content and fatty acid composition. Feed intake and average daily gains did not differ by dietary treatments and no statistically differences were noted. The most substantial (P<0.05) increase in unsaturated fatty acids concentration, including conjugated isomers of C18:2, was obtained in all fat depots in lambs fed LSO/FO diet. We can conclude that oil rich in linoleic acid given in a mixture with fish oil may be a good supplement to ruminant diets that enhance fatty acid composition of received product, i.e. meat.
