TI  - THE EFFECT OF SEASON ON THE LEVEL OF THYROID-HORMONES AND INSULIN IN THE BLOOD-SERUM OF CALVES
T2  - ZIVOCISNA VYROBA
AB  - The trials were effected on calves of the Holstein-Friesian breed.  45 calves were included in two groups on the random basis, namely 20 calves in the first group and 24 in the second one, at the age of two months, with the similar proportion of sex.  The first group was established in January and second one in June.  We evaluated the average levels of insulin, thyroxine (T4), triiodothyronine (T3) and the T3/T4 ratio between the two groups in the calves of the same age.  Further more the values between January (the 1st group) and June (the 2nd group) at the age of 2 months, between February (the 1st group) and July (the 2nd group) at the age of 3 months, between March (the 1st group) and August (the 2nd group) at the age of 4 months, between the end of March and beginning of April (March/April, the 1st group) and September (the 2nd group) at the age of 5 months, and between April (the 1st group) and October (the 2nd group) at the age of 6 months.
   The nutrition of calves was the same in both the groups.  They were fed milk mixture diluted in 1:10 ratio in the amount of 6-8 1 per day.  Simultaneously they were given from 1.0 up to 1.1 kg of the TKS mixture per head/day and hay ad libitum.  The calves were housed in pens per 10 to 15 heads.
   Blood was sampled in month intervals by puncture from vena jugularis.  After centrifuging the clotted blood in tubes at 3000 revolutions per minute for 30 minutes, blood serum samples were frozen in the amount of 1.5 ml and stored at -20-degrees-C.  In blood serum we observed the concentrations of thyroxine (the RIA method T4 of the Institute in Kosice), triiodothyronine (the RIA method T3 of the Institute in Kosice) and insulin (the RIA method - INS-Kit, MJ-96, of the CPDR in Poland).
   As the obtained values of insulin had not the common distribution, before the results being evaluated mathematically and statistically, they were subjected to logarithm calculation (ln x) after Bonczek et al. (1988).  The result were treated by current variation-statistic methods.  The acquired differences of the observed parameters between seasons were tested by help of Student's t-test.
   In different studied periods the average concentration of insulin in the blood serum of calves was in the 2nd group higher than in the calves of the 1st group.  The higher concentration, statistically significant, was recorded at the beginning of autumn (October) in contrast to April (P < 0.001), in August comparing the values of March (P < 0.05), in July comparing the period of February (P < 0.05) and in June comparing the values of insulin in January (P < 0.01) (Tab. I, Fig. 1a).
   When evaluating the effect of season on the T3/T4 ratio as a significant parameter of the conversion of T4 to T3 we found out the significantly higher ratio in October than in April (P < 0.001), in September comparing the period of the end of March and the beginning of April (P < 0.001).  In August comparing March the ratio was higher at the significance level P < 0.2 (Tab. I, Fig. 1b).
   As to T4 very low concentrations were recorded in the blood serum of calves in winter, when the T4 concentration was significantly lower than over the summer months (January-June P < 0.05; February-July P < 0.001; March-August P < 0.01).  In the early spring (the values obtained at the turn of March/April) the T4 concentration was already higher than by the end of summer and the beginning of autumn, while statistically significant level of T4 in blood serum was higher in April comparing October (P < 0.001) (Tab. I, Fig. 1c).
   We recorded a higher level of T3 in the blood serum of calves of the 2nd group in comparison with the 1st group.  Significantly higher were the values of T3 measured at the beginning of autumn in comparison with April (P < 0.05), in September comparing the values of the turn of March/April (P < 0.001) and in July comparing February (P < 0.05).  The higher level of T3 in the blood serum of calves in August in comparison with March was significant at the level P < 0.1 (Tab. I, Fig. 1d).
   The lower levels of thyroid hormones recorded in the blood serum of calves in winter comparing the summer period are probably related to the higher conversion of T4 to T3 (it is demonstrated by the lower level of T4 and the lower values of T3/T4 ratio) as well as to the higher cells demand for T3 (it is demonstrated by the lower level of T3).  The lower concentration of insulin in winter probably seems to reduce the flow of nutrients into the adipose and skeletal tissue in order to provide the energy abundance for covering of the increasing demands for energy in cold environment in comparison with the warm one.  The lower levels of the hormones observed are probably related to the lower proportion of energy for the stimulation of hormone secretion, as the same absolute intake would be smaller in relation to the energy demands in cold environment when the energy demands for thermoregulation increase.
